WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.488
Narrator: The Battle of Winterfell,

00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:04.522
which took place during

00:00:04.522 --> 00:00:07.158
season eight, episode
three, "The Long Night,"

00:00:07.158 --> 00:00:10.128
was the most epic battle
on "Game of Thrones" yet.

00:00:10.128 --> 00:00:12.196
The production was, to say the least,

00:00:12.196 --> 00:00:14.599
a brutal labor of love.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:17.468
Here are just a few of the
most amazing things about it

00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:19.237
and how they were brought to life.

00:00:19.237 --> 00:00:21.239
Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:22.807 --> 00:00:27.378
Over the course of 11 weeks,
a cast and crew of 750 people

00:00:27.378 --> 00:00:30.281
worked in the worst
imaginable weather conditions,

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:31.849
battling freezing temperatures

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:35.052
and all the elements, to make history.

00:00:35.052 --> 00:00:37.855
Director Miguel Sapochnik,
who has directed

00:00:37.855 --> 00:00:40.892
some of the most legendary
"Thrones" episodes to date,

00:00:40.892 --> 00:00:42.093
took the helm here.

00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:46.097
In order to keep off the undead army,

00:00:46.097 --> 00:00:49.400
a giant moat was created
around Winterfell.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.003
The wights famously do not like fire,

00:00:52.003 --> 00:00:56.140
so to keep them away, Melisandre
lights it all on fire.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.943
Yes, the fire you saw was real.

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:01.012
In order to achieve this effect,

00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:03.848
they used about 900 steel logs.

00:01:03.848 --> 00:01:06.617
They chose steel instead of wood

00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:08.186
as they would appear to catch fire

00:01:08.186 --> 00:01:10.788
without completely burning away.

00:01:10.788 --> 00:01:13.191
To make the fire spread
in the first place,

00:01:13.191 --> 00:01:14.392
they filled water troughs

00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:17.795
with gallons and gallons of propane.

00:01:17.795 --> 00:01:20.298
The fire was important
to add light to a battle

00:01:20.298 --> 00:01:22.767
that was mostly shot in the dark of night,

00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.669
a fact that has been controversial

00:01:24.669 --> 00:01:26.470
for "Game of Thrones" fans

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.774
as it made it hard to know
what was happening at times.

00:01:29.774 --> 00:01:32.810
Cinematographer Fabian Wagner told Wired,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.045
"The showrunners decided that

00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:36.013
this had to be a dark episode,"

00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:40.084
and, "Everything they wanted
the audience to see is there."

00:01:41.118 --> 00:01:43.955
A lot of dead bodies
pile up in this episode,

00:01:43.955 --> 00:01:47.892
and the crew used practical
effects whenever they could.

00:01:47.892 --> 00:01:49.961
Many of the wights that
pop up in the crypt

00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:53.397
were heavily inspired by
the look of ancient mummies,

00:01:53.397 --> 00:01:55.199
decaying skin and all.

00:01:55.199 --> 00:01:59.103
The wights were mostly a
combination of prosthetics and CGI.

00:01:59.103 --> 00:02:02.840
Prop dead bodies, meanwhile,
are not easy to handle.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.344
They're expensive, heavy,
and hard to move around.

00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:08.079
The prop makers came up with a trick

00:02:08.079 --> 00:02:10.348
following the Battle of the Bastards.

00:02:10.348 --> 00:02:13.217
Instead of having to stick
individual bodies up,

00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:14.885
they would stack up a mound of dummies

00:02:14.885 --> 00:02:17.455
and create a detailed mold of them.

00:02:17.455 --> 00:02:21.726
The team created around
300 of these body piles.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.743
This episode wasn't going to end

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:26.712
without the death of a fan favorite,

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:28.842
and "The Long Night" gave us the demise

00:02:28.842 --> 00:02:31.869
of Lyanna Mormont, played by Bella Ramsey,

00:02:31.869 --> 00:02:35.164
who sacrifices herself to
kill off the giant wight.

00:02:35.164 --> 00:02:39.539
Achieving this involved
complicated and groundbreaking CGI.

00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:41.781
They shot a piece of the scene on location

00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:43.850
in order to get the backdrop.

00:02:43.864 --> 00:02:45.499
While on set, a crew member

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:48.436
held a giant head on a tall pole.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.085
While this was happening,

00:02:50.085 --> 00:02:52.621
the camera operators were using Incam.

00:02:52.621 --> 00:02:55.343
This technology allowed
them to play back animation

00:02:55.357 --> 00:02:57.826
of what the giant's
performance would look like

00:02:57.826 --> 00:03:00.114
that was then synced to the camera.

00:03:00.114 --> 00:03:03.303
This allowed the camera operators
to actually see the giant

00:03:03.303 --> 00:03:06.158
at his proper scale while on set.

00:03:06.158 --> 00:03:09.728
Then, against a green
screen, actor Ian Whyte,

00:03:09.742 --> 00:03:13.246
who is over 7 feet tall,
actually played the giant.

00:03:13.246 --> 00:03:14.848
He was filmed in a way

00:03:14.848 --> 00:03:18.737
that made him seem like
he was double his size.

00:03:19.145 --> 00:03:21.648
Ramsey wasn't actually on set with him,

00:03:21.648 --> 00:03:23.867
so for the shots where
the giant picks her up,

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:25.652
he instead lifted up a green doll

00:03:25.652 --> 00:03:27.671
covered in tracking markers.

00:03:27.671 --> 00:03:30.223
That scene was then motion-captured.

00:03:30.258 --> 00:03:33.331
In the final layer, for closeup shots,

00:03:33.331 --> 00:03:35.533
Ramsey was lifted up
by a giant robotic arm

00:03:35.533 --> 00:03:40.027
that picked her up and moved
her exactly as the giant would.

00:03:40.027 --> 00:03:42.432
The giant wasn't even close
to the biggest creature

00:03:42.432 --> 00:03:44.404
involved in the battle.

00:03:44.404 --> 00:03:49.344
There were the three dragons,
Rhaegal, Drogon, and Viserion.

00:03:49.344 --> 00:03:51.405
Famously, whenever you see shots of

00:03:51.425 --> 00:03:53.727
Jon and Daenerys riding the dragons,

00:03:53.747 --> 00:03:56.216
in reality, they're actually riding on

00:03:56.216 --> 00:03:58.918
what looks like a big mechanical bull.

00:03:58.918 --> 00:04:01.694
They also actually shoot fire on set.

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:03.562
The rest of what you see on screen

00:04:03.562 --> 00:04:05.945
as well as the color of Viserion's flames

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:08.915
is largely a result of CGI.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:10.917
Animating Viserion breathing fire

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:13.299
differed from the two alive dragons.

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:16.277
Part of that was because
Viserion breathes blue fire,

00:04:16.277 --> 00:04:19.741
but also, this dragon is
injured. He's leaking,

00:04:19.741 --> 00:04:23.058
as VFX supervisor Joe Bauer points out.

00:04:23.392 --> 00:04:27.117
He has a hole in his neck,
and he's missing half his face

00:04:27.117 --> 00:04:30.248
by the time Jon Snow comes
face-to-face with him.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:33.637
You can see fire coming out of
different parts of his face.

00:04:33.637 --> 00:04:35.506
The damage caused by the dragon's fire

00:04:35.506 --> 00:04:37.942
was added by production designers.

00:04:37.942 --> 00:04:39.339
They walked around with flamethrowers

00:04:39.339 --> 00:04:41.360
and covered rubble with dirt and mud.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.185
"After 20 minutes of watching
a battle, you're over it,"

00:04:44.185 --> 00:04:46.759
Sapochnik said to Entertainment Weekly.

00:04:46.759 --> 00:04:50.300
So how do you stop it from
being a battle in that sense?

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.154
The biggest inspiration for this battle

00:04:52.154 --> 00:04:53.307
was the Battle of Helm's Deep

00:04:53.307 --> 00:04:55.795
from the "Lord of the
Rings: The Two Towers,"

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.720
which Sapochnik notes is actually

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.149
three different intercut battles.

00:05:00.149 --> 00:05:01.644
But that doesn't mean the action

00:05:01.652 --> 00:05:04.047
wasn't incredibly important.

00:05:04.047 --> 00:05:06.984
The star of the episode
might've been Maisie Williams,

00:05:06.984 --> 00:05:09.079
whose Arya Stark delivers the fatal blow

00:05:09.079 --> 00:05:12.042
to the Night King that saves Winterfell.

00:05:12.042 --> 00:05:15.045
It was a long road for Williams
leading to this episode.

00:05:15.045 --> 00:05:16.507
She said the director called her

00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:18.936
a year before "The Long
Night" started shooting

00:05:19.141 --> 00:05:21.210
and told her to start training,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.298
but she had no idea what
exactly she was training for.

00:05:25.568 --> 00:05:28.371
To prepare for some of
the set's tight spaces,

00:05:28.371 --> 00:05:29.957
weapons master Tommy Dunne

00:05:29.957 --> 00:05:32.393
gave Arya a staff that would disassemble,

00:05:32.393 --> 00:05:35.355
allowing her to fight in narrow spaces.

00:05:35.355 --> 00:05:38.358
With the exception of some of
the more dangerous moments,

00:05:38.358 --> 00:05:40.586
Williams does most of her own stunts.

00:05:40.586 --> 00:05:43.247
In fact, she's so committed to Arya

00:05:43.247 --> 00:05:44.649
that the right-handed Williams

00:05:44.649 --> 00:05:46.818
learned how to fight left-handed

00:05:46.818 --> 00:05:51.186
just so she could fight the
same way Arya did in the books.

00:05:51.186 --> 00:05:53.115
When she took down the Night King,

00:05:53.115 --> 00:05:56.239
she was attached to a wire
and launched into him.

00:05:56.239 --> 00:05:58.836
That sequence was mostly shot
in front of a green screen

00:05:58.836 --> 00:06:01.823
so the crew could have better
control over the environment.

